Similarities & Differences
Both companies were seeking to improve operational efficiency, but their approaches diverged significantly. 
San Miguel Corporation’s phased, well-planned approach with strong leadership contributed to its success. 
In contrast, Philippine Airlines’ rushed big-bang strategy, lack of training, and technical issues led to its failure.

Critical Success vs. Failure Factors
- Critical Success Factors: Effective leadership, clear goals, phased implementation, and comprehensive training were pivotal to the success of San Miguel Corporation’s ERP implementation.
- Critical Failure Factors: Poor planning, lack of training, resistance from employees, and inadequate customization were the primary reasons for Philippine Airlines’ failure.

Key Takeaways & Lessons Learned
What did the successful case do right?
- San Miguel Corporation’s careful planning, phased rollout, and emphasis on user training and feedback ensured that their ERP implementation was successful.

What went wrong in the failed case?
- Philippine Airlines rushed the implementation process, failing to allocate sufficient time for planning and training, leading to system failure.

How can future implementations avoid similar failures?
- Future implementations should adopt a phased approach, ensure proper training for all employees, involve stakeholders early, and conduct thorough system testing before going live.

Insights & Recommendations
Best Practices for Successful Enterprise System Implementation
Use a phased implementation approach to reduce risk and allow for continuous feedback.
Engage all stakeholders early in the process to ensure that the system meets the needs of users.
Prioritize user training and provide continuous support to ensure smooth adoption of the system.

Strategies to Avoid Failure
Avoid rushing the implementation; allocate sufficient time for planning, testing, and training.
Ensure that the system is properly customized to fit the company’s unique processes.
Maintain clear communication throughout the implementation process to align expectations.

